{"alignments":[],"candidates":[{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"5","reason":"The x-coordinate of 2a+b is 2×2+1=5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_h087_q1","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_081","cnm_018"],"id":"m1","label":"First-coordinate calculation","methodDirectNodeIds":{"m1":["cnm_h087"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h087","cnm_081","cnm_018"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"2","reason":"The y-coordinate of 2a+b is 2×(-1)+4=2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_h087_q2","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_081","cnm_018"],"id":"m1","label":"Second-coordinate calculation","methodDirectNodeIds":{"m1":["cnm_h087"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h087","cnm_081","cnm_018"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 9","reason":"Diagonal bisection gives P + R = Q + S, so S = (-3,-2) + (9,1) - (1,-5) = (5,4). Its coordinate sum is 9.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1963_AMC_12_Q12","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"opposite-side-vectors","label":"Equal opposite-side vectors","methodDirectNodeIds":{"opposite-side-vectors":["cnm_h086","cnm_h087"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h086","cnm_h087"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. Always MH = MK","reason":"Put H=(h,0), K=(k,0), B=(h,b), and C=(k,c). Then M=((h+k)/2,(b+c)/2), so MH^2=MK^2=(h-k)^2/4+(b+c)^2/4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1970_AMC_12_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"coordinate-midpoint","label":"Use midpoint coordinates","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinate-midpoint":["cnm_h087","cnm_h173"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h087","cnm_h173"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_064","cnm_082"],"id":"coordinates","label":"Compare squared distances","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinates":["cnm_055","cnm_h087","cnm_h173"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_h087","cnm_h173","cnm_064","cnm_082"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 120°","reason":"For v_n = A_(n+1) - A_n, the midpoint rule gives v_(n+2) = -v_(n+1) - v_n/2, hence v_(n+4) = -v_n/4. Therefore triples (A43,A44,A45) and (A3,A4,A5) are similar in that order. Since A4A5 is parallel to A1A3, ∠A4A5A3 = 180° - 60° = 120°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1978_AMC_12_Q28","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"vector-recurrence","label":"Derive the four-step vector scaling","methodDirectNodeIds":{"vector-recurrence":["cnm_h087","cnm_275"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h087","cnm_275"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D (50)","reason":"Triangle AA'B' has twice the area of triangle DAB; the analogous statements hold at the other vertices. The four original vertex triangles total twice the quadrilateral's area. Thus the exterior triangles total 4·10, and the enlarged area is 10+40=50.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1978_AMC_12_Q29","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"coordinate-area-identity","label":"Use coordinates and shoelace","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinate-area-identity":["cnm_h087","math:shoelace-formula"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h087","math:shoelace-formula"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (10), with D=(7,3)","reason":"The midpoint sides are half the original diagonals, so AC and BD must be perpendicular and equally long. Since AC=(2,-6), BD must be (6,2) or (-6,-2). These give D=(7,3) or (-5,-1); only the first is in the first quadrant, and 7+3=10.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2001_AMC_12_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"midpoint-coordinate-vectors","label":"Midpoint coordinate vectors","methodDirectNodeIds":{"midpoint-coordinate-vectors":["cnm_h087","cnm_h090"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h087","cnm_h090"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_082","cnm_244"],"id":"midpoint-quarter-turns","label":"Complete the square from a known side","methodDirectNodeIds":{"midpoint-quarter-turns":["cnm_191","cnm_h087","cnm_245","cnm_083"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_191","cnm_h087","cnm_245","cnm_083","cnm_055","cnm_082","cnm_244"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A (1/16)","reason":"After half a perimeter, the particles swap positions and the midpoint locus repeats. Its three vertices are the midpoints of the medians of ABC. Relative to the centroid, each is 1/4 of the corresponding vertex vector. Thus the enclosed triangle has area ratio (1/4)²=1/16.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2007_AMC_12B_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"coordinate-midpoint-trace","label":"Trace midpoint coordinates","nodeIds":["cnm_h087","cnm_084"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_082","cnm_055","cnm_p111"],"id":"solution:coordinate-midpoint-trace:8aa73fe02fd6","label":"Track the turning times with coordinates","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinate-midpoint-trace":["cnm_272","cnm_084","cnm_h087"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_272","cnm_084","cnm_h087","cnm_082","cnm_055","cnm_p111"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 1/2^98","reason":"Writing z_n=a_n+ib_n gives z_(n+1)=(sqrt(3)+i)z_n. Since (sqrt(3)+i)^99=2^99 i, z_1=(4-2i)/2^99, so a_1+b_1=1/2^98. Equivalently, three steps send (a,b) to (-8b,8a).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2008_AMC_12A_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"real-recurrence-blocks","label":"Group the real recurrence into blocks","nodeIds":["cnm_h087","cnm_143"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h197","cnm_h200","cnm_h040"],"id":"solution:real-recurrence-blocks:de9e6e14d179","label":"Group updates into blocks of three","methodDirectNodeIds":{"real-recurrence-blocks":["math:function-composition-iteration","cnm_h087"]},"nodeIds":["math:function-composition-iteration","cnm_h087","cnm_h197","cnm_h200","cnm_h040"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"4","reason":"(1, 2) + (3, 4) = (4, 6), so the x-coordinate is 4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_h081_q1","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_010"],"id":"m1","label":"Corresponding coordinates","methodDirectNodeIds":{"m1":["cnm_h087"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h087","cnm_010"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"6","reason":"The y-coordinate of the sum is 2 + 4 = 6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_h081_q2","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_010"],"id":"m1","label":"Corresponding coordinates","methodDirectNodeIds":{"m1":["cnm_h087"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h087","cnm_010"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"3","reason":"(5, -1) + (-2, 1) = (3, 0), so the x-coordinate is 3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_h081_q3","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_010"],"id":"m1","label":"Corresponding coordinates","methodDirectNodeIds":{"m1":["cnm_h087"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h087","cnm_010"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAns